<h3>Lighting temperature</h3>
<p>2700K–3000K everywhere except utility areas. 3500K in kitchens is acceptable if you need extra task visibility. Anything above 4000K reads as fluorescent-office and destroys the palette instantly.</p>
<h3>Colour combinations that consistently work</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The safe bet:</strong> Cream walls + walnut furniture + mustard accent + tan leather</li>
  <li><strong>The confident version:</strong> Warm oat walls + teak furniture + burnt orange sofa + deep green plants</li>
  <li><strong>The cool-MCM:</strong> Warm white walls + oak furniture + deep teal accent chair + brass fittings</li>
  <li><strong>The moody version:</strong> Warm charcoal accent wall + walnut furniture + cream sofa + mustard cushions</li>
</ul>
<h3>Mistakes to avoid</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Using more than one accent colour in the same room</li>
  <li>Painting all four walls in a saturated colour — always keep three walls neutral</li>
  <li>Mixing cool grey flooring with warm wood furniture (the room fights itself)</li>
  <li>Using 4000K+ lighting — it kills the warmth in seconds</li>
  <li>Ignoring the ceiling — a bright white ceiling above a warm room feels disconnected</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Rule of thumb</span>60 warm neutral / 30 wood / 10 accent. If your accent starts creeping past 15% of the visible room, pull it back — you're heading into 1970s territory, not MCM.</div>
<p>05 · Materials Used</p>
<h2>Every material that belongs in a Mid-Century Modern home</h2>
<p>Material honesty is the soul of this style, so material selection is where budgets are won and lost. Here is the working list we hand our own project managers.</p>
<h3>Walls</h3>
<p>Smooth putty finish + warm emulsion paint is the base. One accent wall in wooden slats (charred oak look, walnut veneer strips or fluted teak) is the single most Mid-Century move you can make. Avoid textured wallpapers and heavy stone cladding. If you want texture, use lime-wash paint or micro-cement in earth tones.</p>
<h3>Flooring</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Option</th><th>Rate (per sqft)</th><th>MCM Fit</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Engineered wood</td><td>₹280–₹550</td><td></td><td>The ideal choice. Warm, stable, refinishable once.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Wood-look SPC / laminate</td><td>₹90–₹180</td><td></td><td>Best-in-class value. Pick warm oak/walnut planks.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Terrazzo tile</td><td>₹180–₹380</td><td></td><td>Very authentic to the era; brilliant in Bangalore climate.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Warm vitrified tile</td><td>₹75–₹160</td><td></td><td>Choose sand/oat tones, matte finish only.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Marble</td><td>₹200–₹1200</td><td></td><td>Not typical; if used, warm travertine — never cool grey.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Furniture materials</h3>
<p>Walnut and teak are the star woods. Rubberwood with walnut veneer is our factory-direct workhorse — it delivers the look at 40% of the solid wood cost with almost none of the warping risk in Bangalore's humidity swings. Rosewood is authentic to the era but too expensive and too regulated to be practical. Cane webbing (viennese cane) on chair backs and cabinet doors is deeply MCM and inexpensive to add.</p>
<h3>Wardrobes and kitchen carcasses</h3>
<p>18mm BWR-grade plywood is our default carcass — we won't use MDF on wardrobes because Bangalore's humidity destroys MDF edges within four years. Shutters can be walnut veneer, PU-finished, or plain matte laminate in cream/oat. Handles are recessed grooves or slim brass pulls — no chunky chrome.</p>
<h3>Countertops</h3>
<p>Quartz in warm off-white or with subtle veining is our top pick — it's food-safe, low-maintenance, and non-porous. Butcher block (real wood) on an island edge is deeply authentic but needs oiling twice a year. Avoid high-gloss granite; avoid stark white marble.</p>
<h3>Glass, metal, stone, tiles, fabric, leather</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Glass:</strong> Clear or lightly smoked. No frosted, no ridged.</li>
  <li><strong>Metal:</strong> Brushed brass, matte black, powder-coated steel. No chrome. No rose gold.</li>
  <li><strong>Stone:</strong> Travertine, warm terrazzo. Sparingly.</li>
  <li><strong>Tiles:</strong> Handmade zellige-style for kitchen splashbacks; small hex or fish-scale for bathrooms.</li>
  <li><strong>Fabric:</strong> Bouclé, tweed, wool blends, natural linen. Avoid synthetic velvet.</li>
  <li><strong>Leather:</strong> Tan, cognac or camel top-grain — one leather piece per room is enough.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Veneer, laminate, paint, wallpaper</h3>
<p>Walnut veneer (0.5mm natural, PU-finished) is the character material. Matte laminates in cream, oat and warm off-white are the workhorses. Paints should be low-VOC emulsions with a matte or eggshell finish — never high-gloss. Wallpaper is used sparingly, ideally with subtle organic patterns; large florals or geometric prints break the style.</p>
