<h3>Maintenance and lifespan</h3>
<p>Walnut veneer with PU finish: 12–15 years before it needs any attention. Engineered wood floor: 15–20 years with one refinish. Quartz countertop: 20+ years, effectively lifetime. Bouclé upholstery: 6–8 years before it starts pilling in high-use zones. Cane webbing: 8–10 years, easily replaceable panel-by-panel. Brass fittings develop a patina — that's a feature, not a defect.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>Factory-direct advantage:</strong> When we build furniture in-house, veneer thickness is 0.7mm instead of the 0.5mm you'll see in retail. Doesn't sound like much — until you refinish the piece ten years later and there's still material left to sand.</div>
<p>06 · Furniture Style</p>
<h2>The furniture vocabulary — every piece, sized for Indian apartments</h2>
<p>Furniture is where Mid-Century Modern lives or dies. Everything else you can adjust; if the furniture is wrong, no amount of paint fixes it. Below is the vocabulary — and the exact dimensions our workshops build to.</p>
<h3>Furniture shapes and heights</h3>
<p>Silhouettes are low, tapered and horizontal. Sofa seat heights sit at 380–420mm (versus 450–480mm for contemporary sofas). Coffee tables sit at 320–380mm. Dining chairs at 440mm. Beds at 380–450mm to the mattress top. The visual effect is that the eye travels horizontally across the room and the ceiling feels taller.</p>
<h3>Sofa</h3>
<p>Three-seater with tapered wooden legs (not skirt-hidden), low back (720–780mm total height), tight or slightly loose seat cushions, and arms that either slope down or stay slim and vertical. Bouclé, wool blend or textured cotton upholstery in oat, camel, olive, rust or deep teal. Avoid: L-shaped modular sectionals (too contemporary), tufted backs (too traditional), skirt bases (too farmhouse).</p>
<h3>Dining</h3>
<p>Round or oval pedestal table (Tulip-style) for compact rooms, rectangular tapered-leg for larger. Solid wood or wood-topped over a black metal frame. Chairs: wishbone-style, spindle-back, or moulded plywood shell. Six-seater is the sweet spot for Bangalore families — eight-seaters need at least a 12x14ft dining area.</p>
<h3>TV unit</h3>
<p>Wall-mounted, floating, or on tapered legs. Length 1800–2400mm, height 350–420mm. Combination of open shelving (for books, records, artefacts) and closed cabinet (for cable management, gadgets). Walnut veneer with brass or matte black pulls. Never a full-height wall unit — that's contemporary, not MCM.</p>
<h3>Coffee table</h3>
<p>Low (320–380mm), organic shape (kidney, oval, rounded rectangle), or Noguchi-inspired sculptural base with glass top. Materials: solid walnut, teak, or terrazzo. A pair of small round side tables often works better in Bangalore living rooms than one large coffee table.</p>
<h3>Wardrobes</h3>
<p>Full-height (2100–2400mm), floor-to-ceiling for storage efficiency, but broken into three or four vertical panels with reveal grooves so the piece reads as furniture, not architecture. Handleless with recessed grooves or slim brass pulls. Internal fitouts: hanging, drawers, shelves; loft storage above with separate shutters. In a 10x12 bedroom, we typically install a 7ft wide wardrobe.</p>
<h3>Beds</h3>
<p>Low platform bed with tapered legs, or a slightly floating base. Headboard is either a low upholstered rectangle (700–850mm above mattress) in bouclé or leather, or a wooden slatted headboard. Avoid: tall four-posters, ornate carved headboards, storage beds with heavy chunky bases (defeats the low-slung look — though hydraulic storage is fine when the base is styled correctly).</p>
<h3>Storage — floating, built-in, modular</h3>
<p><strong>Floating:</strong> TV consoles, bedside tables and vanity units. Mounted 250–350mm off the floor. Visually reduces bulk. <br> <strong>Built-in:</strong> Wardrobes, kitchen cabinetry, study units. Structural storage that disappears into the walls. <br> <strong>Modular:</strong> Bookshelves, credenzas, sideboards. These carry the "furniture-as-object" character of MCM and should never be built-in — a great walnut credenza should be able to move to your next home.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Buying rule</span>Spend on the sofa, the dining table and one statement chair. Save on the coffee table, the TV unit and side tables. This is where our factory-direct model helps — you can invest in the pieces that matter and still keep your total furniture spend under ₹3.5–4 lakh for a full 2BHK.</div>
<h3>Iconic silhouettes to know (and inspired-by alternatives)</h3>
