<p>07 · Kitchen Design</p>
<h2>A Mid-Century Modern kitchen that works in Indian cooking conditions</h2>
<p>The original 1960s American MCM kitchen was designed for baking, salad-making and one-pot dinners — not for daily <em>tadka</em>. What we build for Bangalore homes keeps the MCM vocabulary but respects Indian cooking realities: chimney, cross-ventilation, hidden dry storage, and materials that survive turmeric.</p>
<h3>Cabinets</h3>
<p>Slab-front shutters in matte laminate or PU (cream, oat, warm sage or muted teal for lowers), paired with walnut or teak veneer on select tall units and open shelves. Handleless with recessed grooves is our preferred look. If you want handles, thin brass or matte black pulls — never chunky.</p>
<h3>Countertops</h3>
<p>Warm-tone quartz (subtle veining, off-white base) is the workhorse. 20mm thickness with a slim mitred edge. For homes that cook heavy Indian food daily, we specify a full-height quartz backsplash behind the hob and a stone or ceramic apron in front of the sink — protects the cabinet skirting from splashes.</p>
<h3>Backsplashes</h3>
<p>Handmade zellige tiles in warm cream, terracotta or muted olive create instant MCM character. Alternative: 100x300mm subway tiles in matte finish, or a continuous quartz slab that reads as one seamless surface. Avoid glossy 3D tiles — kills the style.</p>
<h3>Handles, lighting, hardware</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Handles:</strong> Recessed grooves default; slim brass or matte black pulls (75–128mm) if you must have hardware</li>
  <li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Under-cabinet warm LED strip (2700K, 8W/m), a statement pendant over the breakfast counter (rattan, brass or milk glass), warm LED ceiling downlights at the perimeter</li>
  <li><strong>Hardware:</strong> Hettich or Blum soft-close hinges and drawer slides — this is non-negotiable, buy the best tier of hardware even if you compromise elsewhere</li>
</ul>
<h3>Storage strategy</h3>
<p>Deep drawers instead of shutters below the counter — heavier cookware sits at hand-height, back never bends double. Tall units (2100mm+) for pantry storage with pull-out wireware. Corner units use L-shape rotating carousels or magic corners (blind corners waste 8–10 cubic feet of space in every Indian kitchen). Overhead cabinets: two-tier — lower shelf for daily crockery, upper for seasonal storage.</p>
<h3>Tall units and corner units</h3>
<p>A tall pantry unit near the entry to the kitchen — 600mm wide × 2100mm tall × 600mm deep — holds a family's dry provisions comfortably. Corner units on the L or U layout use pull-out mechanisms; never leave a blind corner in a Bangalore kitchen because the moisture will destroy anything stored back there.</p>
<h3>Island and breakfast counter</h3>
<p>Islands only work in kitchens over 140 sqft with a minimum 1050mm clearance on all sides. In most Bangalore apartments, a breakfast counter (extending from the platform, 600mm deep × 900mm high) is the smarter choice — it delivers 80% of the MCM aesthetic without eating up walking room. Pair with two stools in cane or moulded plywood.</p>
<h3>Appliances</h3>
<p>Chimney: 90cm, auto-clean, 1150m³/hr suction minimum for Indian cooking. Hob: 4-burner glass or matte black. Built-in oven and microwave in the tall unit column at eye-height. Refrigerator: side-by-side or French door in stainless steel matte finish — the retro fridge look is charming but service network in India is thin, so we don't recommend it unless you're a true enthusiast.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span>"</p>
<h4>Designer's take</h4>
<p><strong>The most successful MCM kitchens we've built keep the visible palette to three materials only:</strong> one wood, one solid colour, one stone. Everything else — appliances, hardware, backsplash — supports these three. This restraint is the entire look.</div>
<p>08 · Bedroom Design</p>
<h2>Bedrooms that whisper — MCM as a sleep environment</h2>
<p>The bedroom is where Mid-Century Modern is at its calmest. Everything works together to reduce visual noise: low bed, warm wood, dim lighting, minimal decor, no busy patterns. The goal is a room that helps you fall asleep in eight minutes instead of forty.</p>
<h3>Beds</h3>
<p>Platform bed with tapered legs, 380–450mm to mattress top. Frame in walnut, teak or rubberwood-with-walnut-veneer. Storage: hydraulic king-size storage is fine (Indian homes need it), just make sure the base doesn't visually overwhelm — a 100mm skirt reveal at the floor keeps the piece breathing. Skip solid-front box beds; they look heavy and un-MCM.</p>
<h3>Wardrobes</h3>
<p>Handleless, floor-to-ceiling, broken into 3–4 vertical panels. Combination of matte laminate and walnut veneer — an all-wood wardrobe becomes too heavy in an average bedroom. Mirror: a full-height panel on one shutter (never a separate mirror on the outside). Internal LED strips activate when opened.</p>
<h3>Headboards</h3>
<p>Two options work: (1) low upholstered rectangle in bouclé, wool or tan leather, 700–850mm above mattress, wall-mounted; (2) horizontal wooden slats in walnut, floor-to-ceiling-height with a subtle spot at the top for wall-wash lighting. Avoid tall padded headboards with buttons — that's contemporary luxury, not MCM.</p>
<h3>Lighting</h3>
<p>Layered as always. Warm ceiling downlights at 2700K on the perimeter, a pendant or wall sconce on either side of the bed (frees up bedside table space), a soft floor lamp in the reading corner, LED strip inside the wardrobe. Never a bright central chandelier.</p>
<h3>Side tables</h3>
<p>Tapered-leg wooden side tables with one drawer and one open shelf, 450–500mm tall (match to mattress height so you can reach without stretching). Round-topped side tables also work in tight corners. Nothing over-designed — the side table shouldn't compete with the bed.</p>
<h3>Dressing units</h3>
<p>A wall-hung floating vanity with a walnut-veneer top, cane-front drawers, and a round wall-mounted mirror. Bulb-lit Hollywood mirrors are not MCM — save those for a glam contemporary bedroom. Dressing unit width: 900–1200mm is enough. Bench in front should be a small tapered-leg stool.</p>
<h3>Storage</h3>
<p>Loft above the wardrobe with separate shutters (for luggage, seasonal quilts). Under-bed hydraulic storage. One credenza or low chest of drawers along an empty wall — this is where MCM's furniture-as-storage philosophy shines. Total storage in a 10x12 MCM bedroom should be around 90–110 cubic feet, all discreetly integrated.</p>
<h3>Soft furnishings</h3>
<p>Bedding in warm neutrals (oat, cream, camel, sage). Layered — a lightweight duvet plus one throw at the foot. Cushions: 2 large square + 2 medium rectangle + 1 lumbar. Rug: wool or wool-cotton blend, warm tones, sized so at least 600mm extends beyond the bed on three sides. Curtains: floor-to-ceiling, warm linen or linen-blend, off-white with matching blackout lining behind.</p>
<p>09 · Living Room Design</p>
<h2>The MCM living room — where the style earns its reputation</h2>
<p>The living room is where every principle of Mid-Century Modern is on display — low furniture, warm materials, layered lighting, sculptural accents. Get this room right and the rest of the home follows.</p>
<h3>TV unit</h3>
<p>Floating wall unit or credenza on tapered legs, 1800–2400mm wide, 350–420mm tall. Walnut veneer with brass detailing, combination of closed cabinet and open shelving. TV is wall-mounted above; the credenza sits below with 200–250mm of clearance to the TV. Cable management is routed inside — no visible wires.</p>
<h3>Display units</h3>
<p>Modular open shelving (think Frank Albini or Nelson Basic Cabinet Series) in walnut on a slim black frame. Used for a mix of books, ceramics, small artefacts, and one or two coffee-table books lying flat. Never over-fill — the empty shelf space is doing design work.</p>
<h3>Feature wall and wall panels</h3>
<p>The single most impactful MCM move: a full-height wooden slat feature wall behind the sofa or TV. Vertical fluted panels in walnut veneer, 40–60mm wide, running floor-to-ceiling. Cost: ₹1200–₹1800 per sqft. Alternative: a warm-toned lime-wash or micro-cement wall with a large piece of framed abstract art.</p>
<h3>False ceiling</h3>
<p>Simple. A peripheral cove with warm LED strip, or a single-level flat ceiling with recessed spotlights, or a wooden slat detail running above the sofa. Multi-tier gypsum ceilings with mirrored insets are the fastest way to break the style. If your builder installed a heavy false ceiling, cover it with wooden slats or repaint — don't try to work with it.</p>
<h3>Lighting</h3>
<p>Three layers: (1) ambient — warm 2700K downlights and cove LED, (2) accent — a floor lamp beside the sofa, a table lamp on the credenza, (3) statement — a sculptural pendant or a Nelson-style ball pendant over the coffee table. Warm dimmers on every switch. This one detail elevates a good MCM living room to a great one.</p>
<h3>Sofa</h3>
<p>See Section 6, but placement matters equally: pull the sofa 200–300mm away from the wall (creates depth and breathing room), and always float a rug under the front two feet at minimum. If the room is compact, a two-and-half-seater with an accent chair beats a full three-seater every time.</p>
<h3>Curtains</h3>
<p>Floor-to-ceiling, mounted 150–200mm above the window frame, extending 200mm beyond on each side. Warm linen or linen-blend in off-white/oat. Never patterned. Blackout lining installed as a second layer behind the sheer for bedrooms; sheer-only in living rooms unless privacy demands otherwise.</p>
<h3>Decor</h3>
<p>Three rules: sculptural (not decorative), few (not many), tactile (not shiny). One large abstract art piece, one sculptural ceramic vessel, one indoor plant with an architectural leaf (fiddle leaf fig, rubber plant, monstera), and one coffee-table book stack. Stop. Don't add. If you need to fill space, you have the wrong furniture layout.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Common trap</span>Clients often ask us to add "one more thing" — a console, a bench, an extra chair. The MCM living room is complete when there's still 30% empty floor. If you have to walk carefully around your furniture, you have too much.</div>
<p>10 · Dining Area</p>
<h2>The dining area — MCM's most social room</h2>
<p>The dining room in Mid-Century Modern is famously convivial. A round or oval table centres the group, tapered chair legs let light travel through, and a single statement pendant hangs like a punctuation mark above.</p>
<h3>Furniture</h3>
<p>Table: pedestal Tulip-style (round/oval, 900–1200mm dia) for 4–6 seaters, or rectangular tapered-leg (1600–2100mm long) for 6–8. Solid wood top or wood-topped over a black metal or fibre pedestal. Chairs: 4 to 6 wishbone, spindle-back or moulded plywood shells — mixing chair styles from the same era is deeply MCM, not chaotic. A bench on one side saves space and encourages family seating in Indian homes.</p>
<h3>Lighting</h3>
<p>One statement pendant over the table, hung so the bottom of the shade is 750–850mm above the tabletop. Materials: rattan, brass, milk glass or moulded acrylic. Nelson bubble lamps are the reference. Warm 2700K bulb, dimmable. Skip chandeliers with crystals — completely wrong style.</p>
<h3>Storage</h3>
<p>A walnut credenza or sideboard along one wall — 1500–2000mm wide, 450mm deep, 800mm tall — holds crockery, table linen and serveware. Top surface is used for a bar setup or seasonal decor. Never a floor-to-ceiling display unit — kills the horizontal flow.</p>
<h3>Crockery units</h3>
<p>Integrated inside the credenza with soft-close drawers and vertical dividers for plates. A separate glass-door crockery cabinet is fine if the room is large enough, styled as a mid-century vitrine on tapered legs with clear or lightly smoked glass.</p>
<h3>Decor</h3>
<p>A centrepiece — a low ceramic bowl of fresh fruit or a small sculptural vase with one dried branch — is enough. Add a linen table runner in a solid warm tone for dinners. That's the entire visual grammar. Anything more starts to look staged.</p>
<p>11 · Bathroom Style</p>
<h2>MCM bathrooms — retro references, modern plumbing</h2>
<p>Bathrooms are where designers get lazy with Mid-Century — either they ignore the style entirely (bathroom looks contemporary while rest of the home is MCM) or they overdo it (mint tiles, gold everything, the room feels like a themed set). The middle path is understated retro.</p>
<h3>Tiles</h3>
<p>Fish-scale or small hex mosaic on one accent wall in warm terracotta, olive, muted teal, or oat. Large-format warm-tone tile on the floor (600x1200mm) in a matte finish. Wall tiles in matte cream or warm off-white on the remaining walls. Grout in a shade close to the tile — never bright white.</p>
<h3>Vanity</h3>
<p>Floating vanity in walnut veneer or matte laminate, 900–1200mm wide, mounted 400mm off the floor. Countertop in warm-toned quartz or a solid oiled wood surface (for guest baths only — not master baths where water contact is heavy). Undermount ceramic basin, or a shallow vessel basin sitting on a solid wood top.</p>
<h3>Mirror</h3>
<p>Round mirror with a slim brass or matte black frame, 550–750mm diameter, mounted 200mm above the vanity. Backlit LED optional but the light must be warm. Rectangular arch-top mirrors are also very MCM.</p>
<h3>Storage</h3>
<p>Two drawers under the vanity + wall-mounted open shelf in walnut for towels and toiletries + a mirror cabinet if space allows. Ventilation is critical — dedicate a slim exhaust fan to every bathroom in Bangalore's humidity.</p>
<h3>Lighting</h3>
<p>Warm LED strip along the top of the mirror, warm 2700K ceiling light, and a small wall sconce beside the mirror for softer face lighting. Dimmer switch if your electrician can wire one — makes 6am bathroom visits kinder.</p>
<h3>Accessories</h3>
<p>Brass or matte black tapware, a matching towel rail, a small ribbed glass jar for toothbrushes, and a solid wood bath tray if you have a tub. That's the MCM bathroom's entire prop list.</p>
<p>12 · Lighting Design</p>
<h2>Lighting is where MCM homes actually happen</h2>
<p>Nothing kills a Mid-Century Modern home faster than bad lighting. Nothing elevates it faster than great lighting. Here is the exact system we specify.</p>
<h3>Natural light</h3>
<p>MCM assumes light. Keep window treatments thin and light-passing. If you have blackout requirements, install a two-layer system — sheer + blackout — so you can control both privacy and light quality. Never install thick brocades or heavy curtains that block the day.</p>
<h3>Artificial light — the four-layer stack</h3>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Fixture Type</th><th>Kelvin / Lumens</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Ambient</td><td>General room fill</td><td>Recessed downlights, cove strip</td><td>2700K / 300–400 lm each</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Task</td><td>Reading, cooking, prep</td><td>Table lamp, under-cabinet, desk lamp</td><td>3000K / 500–800 lm</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Accent</td><td>Highlight art, texture</td><td>Wall sconce, picture light, spotlight</td><td>2700K / 200–400 lm</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Decorative</td><td>Sculptural presence</td><td>Statement pendant, floor lamp</td><td>2700K / 400–800 lm, dimmable</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<h3>Recommended Kelvin values by room</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Living room:</strong> 2700K throughout, all fixtures on dimmers</li>
  <li><strong>Bedroom:</strong> 2700K only; a 3000K reading spot beside the bed is fine</li>
  <li><strong>Kitchen:</strong> 2700K ambient, 3000–3500K under-cabinet task lights</li>
  <li><strong>Bathroom:</strong> 2700K ambient, 3000K mirror light</li>
  <li><strong>Study / home office:</strong> 3000K ambient, 4000K on the desk task lamp only</li>
  <li><strong>Utility / balcony:</strong> 3500K–4000K acceptable</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>Ninety-second test:</strong> Turn off all lights except the ambient layer. Can you still read a book comfortably? If yes, you have too much ambient. If no, your task and accent layers are doing the work — as they should.</div>
<p>13 · Decorative Elements</p>
<h2>Decor — a strict, curated list</h2>
<p>MCM decor is a discipline in restraint. Every piece is chosen, not accumulated. Here's what belongs.</p>
<h3>Plants</h3>
<p>Architectural leaves only — fiddle leaf fig, monstera deliciosa, rubber plant (ficus elastica), snake plant, bird of paradise, ZZ plant. Planted in ceramic pots in warm cream, terracotta or matte black, on tapered wooden plant stands. One large statement plant per room beats three small ones every time.</p>
<h3>Wall art</h3>
<p>Abstract geometric prints, botanical line drawings, mid-century poster reproductions (Josef Frank, Alexander Girard, Saul Bass) in slim walnut or brass frames. One large piece (900x1200mm or larger) above the sofa, or a curated pair. Gallery walls with 15 tiny frames are not MCM — they're contemporary/eclectic.</p>
<h3>Curtains and carpets</h3>
<p>Curtains: covered in Section 9. Carpets: wool or wool-cotton blend in warm neutrals. Solid, or with subtle geometric pattern in tone-on-tone. Berber-style flatweaves work beautifully. Persian or ornate patterned rugs belong in traditional, not MCM.</p>
<h3>Artefacts and decor pieces</h3>
<p>Ceramic vessels, brass sculpture, a wooden bowl on the coffee table, a globe on the credenza. Handcrafted items with visible craftsmanship read as MCM instantly. Mass-produced glossy decor breaks the illusion.</p>
<h3>Metal accents</h3>
<p>Brushed brass and matte black are the MCM metals. Chrome and rose gold are not. Keep metal accents consistent throughout the home — pick one primary (usually brass) and one secondary (matte black), and stay with them.</p>
<h3>Books, mirrors, textures</h3>
<p>Coffee-table books stacked flat or standing on the credenza — always with content that matches the room's mood (design, travel, architecture). Mirrors in round or arch shapes, brass or walnut framed. Textures: rely on the natural texture of materials — the grain of wood, the weave of bouclé, the ridge of cane — rather than added surface treatments.</p>
