<h3>Direct duels</h3>
<h4>Modern vs Contemporary vs Mid-Century Modern</h4>
<p><strong>Modern</strong> is a specific historical movement (roughly 1920–1970) that emphasises function, straight lines, and minimal ornament. <strong>Contemporary</strong> refers to whatever "current" style is — it shifts every 5–7 years. <strong>Mid-Century Modern</strong> is a subset of Modern focused on 1945–1969, with more warmth (wood, curves, colour) than pure Modern. In practical terms: Modern feels colder, Contemporary feels newer, Mid-Century feels warmer.</p>
<h4>Minimalist vs Scandinavian vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Minimalist</strong> is about reduction — remove until only essentials remain. <strong>Scandinavian</strong> is minimalism with cosiness (hygge) — same restraint but warmer materials, lighter woods. <strong>MCM</strong> is Scandinavian's louder American cousin — richer wood tones, bolder accent colours, more sculptural furniture. Storage: Minimalist hides everything; Scandinavian keeps a curated few in view; MCM celebrates the credenza and open shelving as design objects.</p>
<h4>Industrial vs Rustic vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Industrial</strong> is exposed brick, concrete, black metal, edison bulbs — factory-loft aesthetic. <strong>Rustic</strong> is reclaimed wood, wrought iron, natural stone — farmhouse aesthetic. <strong>MCM</strong> is refined wood, brushed brass, curated silhouettes — designer-studio aesthetic. Industrial is cool-toned; Rustic is warm-toned but heavy; MCM is warm-toned and light.</p>
<h4>Japandi vs Scandinavian vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Japandi</strong> fuses Japanese wabi-sabi with Scandinavian minimalism — earthy, imperfect, deeply serene. <strong>Scandinavian</strong> is brighter, more optimistic, more decorative. <strong>MCM</strong> is warmer than both, uses stronger accents, and has more furniture personality. Japandi is monastic; Scandinavian is homey; MCM is expressive within restraint.</p>
<h4>Luxury vs Modern vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Luxury</strong> style leans on statement materials (marble, gold, velvet), scale (double-height, oversized furniture) and finish (high-gloss, mirrored). <strong>Modern</strong> is quieter, cleaner-lined and less material-heavy. <strong>MCM</strong> is materially rich but visually calmer than Luxury — you feel the wealth without seeing it announced.</p>
<h4>Traditional vs Transitional vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Traditional</strong> uses carved wood, upholstery with skirt bases, wallpapers with prints, chandeliers — timeless but heavy. <strong>Transitional</strong> softens Traditional with clean-lined furniture and muted palettes. <strong>MCM</strong> is more angular and less ornamented than both — Transitional is a bridge, MCM is a destination.</p>
<h4>Mediterranean vs Coastal vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Mediterranean</strong> is arched openings, terracotta tile, wrought iron, olive and ochre tones. <strong>Coastal</strong> is white, blue, natural fibre, weathered wood, breezy. <strong>MCM</strong> is more restrained than both and less regionally specific — it fits anywhere in the world.</p>
<h4>Bohemian vs Eclectic vs MCM</h4>
<p><strong>Bohemian</strong> layers texture, pattern, plants, textiles from multiple cultures — warm chaos. <strong>Eclectic</strong> is a curated mix of styles from different periods. <strong>MCM</strong> is disciplined — one clear grammar, no mixing. Boho renters love MCM as their eventual "grown-up" style.</p>
