<h4>Designer's verdict</h4>
<p>If you're between MCM and Scandinavian, ask whether you gravitate toward walnut or oak — walnut answers Mid-Century, oak answers Scandinavian. If you're between MCM and Japandi, ask whether you want the room to feel <strong>curated</strong> (MCM) or <strong>meditative</strong> (Japandi). Simple lens, correct choice every time.</div>
<p>24 · Common Mistakes</p>
<h2>Everything we've seen go wrong (and how to avoid it)</h2>
<h3>Wrong materials</h3>
<p>Mixing high-gloss laminate with matte walnut. Choosing yellow-tinted teak veneer that looks orange under warm lighting. Using PVC edge banding that reveals the substrate at the seam within a year. Selecting MDF wardrobes in Bangalore's humidity (they will fail).</p>
<h3>Wrong lighting</h3>
<p>4000K or higher throughout the home — turns the warmth cold. Only ceiling downlights, no layering. RGB colour-changing LEDs (they announce themselves as "modern" but destroy the palette). Under-illuminating the kitchen because "MCM is moody" — actually, MCM kitchens need excellent task lighting, just at the right temperature.</p>
<h3>Wrong furniture</h3>
<p>Buying tapered legs that are too thin for the piece (they crack). Overscaling — a huge L-shape sectional in a Bangalore apartment kills the horizontal flow. Fabric choices that don't survive daily use — bouclé in a home with two toddlers.</p>
<h3>Wrong paint</h3>
<p>Painting an accent wall in stark cobalt blue or emerald green — reads Contemporary, not MCM. Choosing a warm off-white with pink undertones instead of yellow undertones — makes wood look weird. High-gloss finish on walls — no MCM room uses gloss walls.</p>
<h3>Wrong flooring</h3>
<p>Cool grey vitrified tile in a warm-wood home — the room fights itself daily. Herringbone flooring in a small apartment — pattern overpowers the furniture. Marble in cold veined pattern — completely wrong style.</p>
<h3>Wrong accessories</h3>
<p>Too many objects on every surface. Buying "MCM starburst clocks" from Amazon that read as costume. Mixing brass with chrome and rose gold in the same room. Adding decor after the room is complete because "it feels empty" — that empty space is doing work; trust it.</p>
<h3>Over-decoration</h3>
<p>The classic client instinct: "Let's add one more thing." One more art print. One more cushion. One more plant. MCM is a subtractive style — every addition after the intended composition dilutes it. Restrain yourself for six months post-move-in before deciding what to add.</p>
<h3>Poor space planning</h3>
<p>Placing furniture along walls (this is a rental-apartment reflex, not a design decision). Blocking natural light paths with tall pieces. Not accounting for door swing arcs. Making the walkway between sofa and coffee table less than 450mm — traps everyone.</p>
<p>25 · Designer Tips</p>
<h2>Twenty tips from twenty years of Mid-Century Modern projects</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Buy the sofa before you paint.</strong> Match wall colours to the upholstery you actually chose, not to swatches you imagined.</li>
  <li><strong>Never install a 4000K bulb inside a Mid-Century home.</strong> Ever. Not even in the utility room.</li>
  <li><strong>Rug rule:</strong> at least front two feet of every sofa/chair must sit on the rug. Otherwise the rug reads as an accessory, not architecture.</li>
  <li><strong>Curtains touch the floor.</strong> Puddling by 10–15mm is elegant; floating 30mm above the floor is a rental-apartment tell.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy one iconic piece.</strong> Even at a licensed replica level. Every MCM home should have one clear "hero" that the room revolves around.</li>
  <li><strong>Don't paint the ceiling stark white.</strong> Use half a shade lighter than the walls — connects the room together.</li>
  <li><strong>Plants earn their place.</strong> One large architectural plant beats seven small succulents in every room, every time.</li>
  <li><strong>The credenza is the workhorse.</strong> Buy the best one you can afford — it stores, displays, and anchors the room.</li>
  <li><strong>Warm dimmers everywhere.</strong> Especially living, dining, bedrooms. Non-negotiable at the wiring stage.</li>
  <li><strong>Skip the gallery wall.</strong> One large piece of art beats twelve small frames in this style.</li>
  <li><strong>Coffee-table books do design work.</strong> Choose books that match the room's mood.</li>
  <li><strong>Brass patinas beautifully.</strong> Don't polish it every month; let it develop character.</li>
  <li><strong>Match wood undertones across the room.</strong> All woods should read yellow-warm or red-warm — never mix.</li>
  <li><strong>Handleless is a commitment.</strong> If you're going handleless, do it consistently across kitchen, wardrobes and TV unit.</li>
  <li><strong>Storage first, style second.</strong> Design your storage to actually hold your life, then style over it.</li>
  <li><strong>Cushion trick:</strong> one plain, one textured, one patterned. Three cushions per seat maximum.</li>
  <li><strong>Layer curtains.</strong> Sheer inside, opaque or blackout outside. Gives daytime softness and nighttime privacy.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose one hardware finish and stay.</strong> Brass or matte black. Don't mix within the same room.</li>
  <li><strong>Invest in one bespoke piece.</strong> A custom credenza or headboard elevates the whole home.</li>
  <li><strong>Live in the room before you finish it.</strong> After the modular is done, give yourself 4–6 weeks before choosing decor. You'll know what's missing.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Cost-saving ideas</span>Spend on: sofa, dining table, one statement chair, kitchen hardware, lighting. <br> Save on: coffee table, TV unit, side tables, decor. Factory-direct workshops (like ours) can build the "save" pieces to look identical to premium retail at 40–50% of the cost.</div>
