<h3>Common misconceptions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>"MCM is always mustard and teal."</strong> False. The style is warm-neutral first. Accents can be any of six colour families.</li>
  <li><strong>"MCM is expensive."</strong> False. Factory-direct MCM starts at ₹1,600/sqft — mid-range, not luxury pricing.</li>
  <li><strong>"MCM doesn't work in small apartments."</strong> False. It's arguably the best small-apartment style because of horizontal flow.</li>
  <li><strong>"MCM will look dated soon."</strong> False. It's been in continuous fashion for 20 years and is more mainstream today than five years ago.</li>
  <li><strong>"MCM is minimalist."</strong> Partially false. MCM is <em>restrained</em> — but it's warmer, more expressive, and more materially rich than pure minimalism.</li>
  <li><strong>"I need iconic Eames pieces for real MCM."</strong> False. Well-made replicas from factory-direct workshops deliver the aesthetic at 10–15% of the original cost.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Professional warnings</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Never use MDF for wardrobes in Bangalore.</strong> The humidity swings will destroy edges within 4 years, no matter what the salesperson claims.</li>
  <li><strong>Never accept 4000K or higher lighting.</strong> If your electrician insists it's for "brightness," insist on higher-lumen 2700K instead.</li>
  <li><strong>Never let a designer up-sell decor before the modular is installed.</strong> Live with the space first, decide decor after.</li>
  <li><strong>Never mix hardware finishes.</strong> Once you pick brass or matte black, stay committed across the entire home.</li>
  <li><strong>Never sign a contract without an itemised BOQ.</strong> Lump-sum contracts hide substitution risks.</li>
  <li><strong>Never install cove lighting without a dimmer.</strong> The mood shifts entirely between full and half brightness — you want that control.</li>
</ul>
